    "lawCode": "Add §236-b, County L",
    "lawSection": "County Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8580\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the county law, in relation to providing\nfor the licensure of electrical inspectors within the county of West-\nchester\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL:  This legislation authorizes West-\nchester County to establish a county board of examiners for electrical\ninspectors. The board of examiners would be empowered to assume all\nlicensing duties within Westchester County with respect to the licensure\nof electrical inspectors. This authorization supersedes any inconsistent\ngeneral, special or local law to the contrary.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:  Section one amends the county law by\nadding a new section 236-b.  Subdivision 1 of section 236-b provides\nthat notwithstanding any inconsistent general, special, or local law to\nthe contrary, the county of Westchester is authorized to establish a\nWestchester county board of examiners for electrical inspectors and to\nempower such board to assume all licensing duties within the county of\nWestchester with respect to the licensure of electrical inspectors. The\ncounty of Westchester shall provide for electrical inspectors' duties\nand responsibilities with respect to applications for a license or\nrenewal in accordance with the local law of such county. A local law\nenacted pursuant to this section establishing such county licensure\nshall provide standards for licensure which shall include, at a minimum,\na provision that no person shall obtain a license unless such person\nshall have received certain training which is at least equivalent to the\ntraining required for code enforcement personnel under the rules and\nregulations promulgated by the governor pursuant to article six-C of the\nexecutive law.\n\nSubdivision 2 of section 236-b provides that a local law enacted pursu-\nant to this section establishing county licensure of electrical inspec-\ntors shall supersede any provision requiring electrical inspectors to\nalso obtain a local license promulgated by a city, town or village in\nthe county pursuant to any general, special or local law. Nothing in\nthis section is deemed to supersede any of the powers, functions and\nduties of the fire fighting and code enforcement personnel standards and\neducation commission, as set forth in article six-C of the executive\nlaw.\n\nSubdivision 3 of section 236-b provides a definition of the term \"elec-\ntrical inspector.\"\n\nSubdivision 4 of section 238-b provides a definition of the term \"agency\nof the state of New York.\"\n\nSection two of the legislation provides the effective date, which is\nimmediate.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Many local governments within Westchester County have\napproved and designated electrical inspectors to conduct electrical\n\ninspections in accordance with their enforcement obligations under the\nUniform Fire Prevention and Building Code Act. As a result of such local\nregulation, Westchester County lacks uniform countywide licensing, qual-\nification and performance standards for electrical inspectors.  Effi-\ncient oversight and monitoring designed to benefit the entire county is\nlacking.\n\nAccordingly, the licensing of electrical inspectors by Westchester Coun-\nty would protect the public health, safety and welfare of the county's\nresidents by assuring that electrical inspections are performed only by\nqualified individuals. To be qualified and thus obtain a county license,\nsuch individuals would have to have received training in the inspection\nof electrical components, equipment and systems used in buildings and\nstructures which is at least equivalent to the training in the\ninspection of electrical components, equipment and systems used in\nbuildings and structures required for code enforcement personnel under\nthe rules and regulations promulgated by the governor pursuant to arti-\ncle six-C of the executive law.\n\nThe regulation of electrical inspectors at the county level would\nprovide uniformity of licensing, qualification and performance standards\nthroughout the county and remove the burden on local municipalities of\nmonitoring the industry. Centralized oversight with the potential for\ndisciplinary proceedings that could lead to a license suspension and/or\na revocation would assist in ensuring that electrical inspections are\nperformed diligently, honestly and professionally. Currently, incompe-\ntent electrical inspectors may be prevented from inspecting only one\njurisdiction at a time. Under a centralized oversight scheme, incompe-\ntent electrical inspectors may be prevented from inspecting on a county-\nwide basis.\n\nWestchester County already has exclusive licensing authority over master\nelectricians pursuant New York State County Law section 236-a.  Since\nelectrical inspectors are required to examine the work done by master\nelectricians licensed by the county, it is consistent and sensible to\nalso vest the county with exclusive licensing authority over the elec-\ntrical inspectors themselves.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  A10413/S7322 of 2007-2008 (passed both houses).\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  None to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
